@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+schema_version: '1.0'
+id: agent-self-declaration
+title: Agent Self-Declaration
+description: |
+  Open a task by declaring the agent's identity, scope, and hard constraints, and close it by confirming what was done against that declaration.
+entry_condition: |
+  An agent is about to begin substantive work on a task.
+exit_condition: |
+  The agent declared its role, scope, and hard constraints before working, and at completion confirmed what was done against the declaration, including that each hard constraint was honored.
+steps:
+- title: Declare identity and scope
+  description: |
+    Open with a short declaration of the agent's identity/role and the scope of the task it is about to perform, before substantive work begins.
+- title: Name hard constraints
+  description: |
+    Name any hard constraint that will govern the work (a validation gate that cannot be skipped, content it will never produce or publish, a condition under which it will halt) as part of that declaration.
+- title: Confirm completion against the declaration
+  description: |
+    At completion, confirm what was actually done against what was declared, including confirming that any hard constraint was honored.
diff --git a/packs/built-in/procedures/audience-oriented-drafting.procedure.yaml b/packs/built-in/procedures/audience-oriented-drafting.procedure.yaml
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..f1e20ee
--- /dev/null
+++ b/packs/built-in/procedures/audience-oriented-drafting.procedure.yaml
@@ -0,0 +1,22 @@
+schema_version: '1.0'
+id: audience-oriented-drafting
+title: Audience-Oriented Drafting
+description: |
+  Draft an artifact for a named reader: identify the persona first, name it in or beside the artifact, calibrate to it, and split into persona-specific variants when audiences genuinely diverge.
+entry_condition: |
+  An artifact meant for human readers is about to be drafted.
+exit_condition: |
+  The target persona is identified and named alongside the artifact, the vocabulary, detail, and structure are calibrated to it, and genuinely divergent audiences are served by persona-specific variants.
+steps:
+- title: Identify the target audience first
+  description: |
+    Identify the target audience/persona for the artifact before drafting - their role, their existing familiarity with the subject, and what decision or action the artifact should support.
+- title: Name the target persona explicitly
+  description: |
+    Name the target persona explicitly in or alongside the artifact (a heading, a metadata field, or an opening line) so a later reader can confirm the tailoring was intentional.
+- title: Calibrate to the persona
+  description: |
+    Calibrate vocabulary, level of detail, and structure to that persona - do not default to the author's own level of familiarity with the subject.
+- title: Prefer persona-specific variants
+  description: |
+    When a single source artifact must serve genuinely divergent audiences, prefer producing persona-specific variants over one artifact that tries to serve everyone and therefore serves no one well.
