+schema_version: '1.0'
+id: black-box-integration-test-design
+title: Black-Box Integration Test Design
+description: |
+  Design an integration test from the outside: choose its responsibility boundary, derive cases from the specification, drive the system only through its published interface with inputs and expected outputs fixed in advance, and assert on observable outputs.
+entry_condition: |
+  An integration test is to be written for a component, service, or system boundary.
+exit_condition: |
+  The test drives the system only through its published external interface, its cases derive from specifications with valid and invalid inputs and expected outputs chosen in advance, and it asserts on observable outputs.
+steps:
+- title: Select the responsibility boundary
+  description: |
+    Select the responsibility boundary for the test using the test-boundaries-by-responsibility tactic before writing the test.
+- title: Derive cases from specifications
+  description: |
+    Derive test cases from specifications, requirements, and design parameters rather than from reading the implementation.
+- title: Drive only the published interface
+  description: |
+    Drive the system under test exclusively through its published external interface (e.g., HTTP request, CLI invocation, message send, public module API).
+- title: Choose inputs and expected outputs in advance
+  description: |
+    Select both valid and invalid inputs and determine the expected output in advance of execution.
+- title: Assert on observable outputs
+  description: |
+    Assert on observable outputs returned across the external interface — responses, status codes, persisted records, emitted events, logs, stored artifacts.
+- title: Apply black-box design techniques
+  description: |
+    Apply standard black-box design techniques where relevant — equivalence partitioning, boundary value analysis, decision table testing, state transition testing, error guessing — to choose inputs.
