+schema_version: '1.0'
+id: bounded-context-alignment
+title: Bounded Context Alignment
+description: |
+  Make naming, structure, and integration choices from inside the bounded context the code belongs to, translate explicitly at context boundaries, and flag violations in review.
+entry_condition: |
+  Code or a design decision is about to be written or reviewed, and it lives in, or crosses, a bounded context.
+exit_condition: |
+  The inhabited context is identified, identifiers follow its ubiquitous language, every boundary crossing has an explicit, documented translation, and red-line violations have been flagged in review.
+steps:
+- title: Identify the bounded context
+  description: |
+    Identify which bounded context the code or design decision inhabits before making naming, structural, or integration choices.
+- title: Use the context's ubiquitous language
+  description: |
+    Use the context's established ubiquitous language for all identifiers, method names, and documentation within that context. Do not introduce generic names (Manager, Handler, Data, Utils) that obscure domain meaning.
+- title: Make boundary translation explicit
+  description: |
+    Whenever a design crosses a context boundary, make the translation explicit. Introduce an Anti-Corruption Layer, adapter, or mapper. Document the boundary crossing and the translation logic.
+- title: Flag red-line violations in review
+  description: |
+    Flag red-line violations during review — same term with different meanings in different contexts, translation logic scattered across files, or shared mutable state between contexts.
