+schema_version: '1.0'
+id: c4-diagram-authoring
+title: C4 Diagram Authoring
+description: |
+  Author architecture diagrams with C4 techniques: zoom in from context only as far as needed, make every diagram self-describing, match detail to the audience, keep diagrams as code, and hold one abstraction level per artifact.
+entry_condition: |
+  An architecture diagram is to be created or revised.
+exit_condition: |
+  Diagrams stop at the level that answers the question, carry titles, legends, described elements, and labelled unidirectional relationships, match their audience, live as version-controlled diagram-as-code, and link across levels.
+steps:
+- title: Start broad and zoom in only where needed
+  description: |
+    Start at the broadest level and zoom in only where detail adds value. Draw the System Context first (the system as one box, its users, and the systems it talks to), then Containers, then Components, and stop at the level that answers the question at hand — do not produce every level speculatively.
+- title: Make every diagram self-describing
+  description: |
+    Give every diagram a title, a legend, typed and described elements, and labelled unidirectional relationships. A box with no description or a double-headed arrow that hides who initiates is a communication failure; run the architecture diagram review checklist before sharing.
+- title: Match detail to the audience
+  description: |
+    Match the level of detail to the audience. Context diagrams for sponsors carry no technology labels; component diagrams for developers do not omit internal boundaries for the sake of tidiness. A diagram that tries to serve two audiences serves neither.
+- title: Keep diagrams as code
+  description: |
+    Keep diagrams as version-controlled diagram-as-code. Author them in Mermaid or PlantUML text so they diff in review and stay beside the code, rather than as exported images that go stale silently.
+- title: Hold one abstraction level per artifact
+  description: |
+    Reason at a single abstraction level per artifact and link across levels rather than inlining. Use the drill-down discipline to keep strategic, architectural, design, and code-level reasoning in their own homes with upward and downward traceability.
