+schema_version: '1.0'
+id: canonical-source-discipline
+title: Canonical Source Discipline
+description: |
+  Work from canonical sources, consolidate split-brain surfaces into one authority instead of adding parity, and treat a documented but missing command as an upstream gap.
+entry_condition: |
+  Work needs a template, skill, or CLI surface, or has found the same concept implemented in more than one place, or a documented command is missing.
+exit_condition: |
+  Canonical surfaces were used instead of prior-mission copies, split-brain surfaces were unified (or escalated and re-scoped when equivalence failed), and any missing command was filed as an upstream gap.
+steps:
+- title: Use canonical sources
+  description: |
+    Rule 1 — Use canonical sources: always use the canonical template, skill, or CLI command surface rather than copying from a prior mission in kitty-specs/ or constructing an ad-hoc substitute. Templates under packs/built-in/missions/<type>/templates/ are the single source of truth, resolved through the charter/doctrine chain. Prior missions are historical snapshots — they drift and must never be used as copy sources.
+- title: Unify split-brain surfaces, not parity
+  description: |
+    Rule 2 — Unification not parity: when a split-brain surface is found — the same concept implemented or stored in more than one location — consolidate to one canonical surface authority. Do NOT add the same behavior to every copy (parity); parity preserves the split-brain. Before consolidating, verify the canonical surface carries equivalent DATA and semantics, not merely a compatible interface — two surfaces may encode different concerns with divergent values; when they do, unification is a data-model merge (plan and scope it as such), and if implementation invalidates the equivalence premise, escalate and re-scope rather than force the consolidation. Exception: do not drop a load-bearing invariant without a migration path proven safe by tests.
+- title: Treat a missing command as a gap
+  description: |
+    Rule 3 — Missing command is a gap: when a CLI command is documented in canonical sources but absent from the running tool, trace its source and file an upstream gap issue. Do not silently hand-roll a substitute. The gap record and escalation to the team are the correct outputs, not a workaround committed to the codebase.
