@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+schema_version: '1.0'
+id: disciplined-refactoring-cycle
+title: Disciplined Refactoring Cycle
+description: |
+  Refactor in a disciplined cycle: name the smell, secure a green baseline, apply one named transformation at a time with tests green after each, and keep structural moves apart from behaviour changes.
+entry_condition: |
+  Code is about to be restructured without intending to change its behaviour.
+exit_condition: |
+  Each move addressed a named smell, ran on a green, covered baseline, was a single named transformation with tests green afterwards, and no behaviour change was mixed into a structural step.
+steps:
+- title: Name the smell first
+  description: |
+    Name the smell first. Before moving any code, state in one line the specific problem the refactor addresses (e.g. 'this method uses another class's data more than its own' or 'this concept is implicit and unnamed'). The named smell selects the transformation; an unnamed one means the move is not yet justified.
+- title: Establish a green baseline
+  description: |
+    Establish a green baseline. Ensure the surface about to change is covered by passing tests before the first move. If coverage is missing, add characterization tests that pin current behaviour first — never refactor under red or uncovered code.
+- title: Apply one transformation at a time
+  description: |
+    Apply one transformation at a time. Perform a single, named refactoring move (extract, encapsulate, move, inline, replace-conditional-with-polymorphism, incremental cutover) and stop. Do not batch several moves into one step.
+- title: Keep tests green after every step
+  description: |
+    Re-run tests after every step and keep them green. A step that reddens the suite is reverted as a unit and re-approached in smaller increments; the tests must be green again before the next move begins.
+- title: Separate structural moves from behaviour changes
+  description: |
+    Keep structural moves separate from behaviour changes. If a behaviour change is needed, finish or park the refactor at a green point and make the behaviour change as its own step with its own tests, so review can trace each diff to either 'same behaviour, better shape' or 'new behaviour'.
