@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+schema_version: '1.0'
+id: minutes-self-sufficiency-check
+title: Minutes Self-Sufficiency Check
+description: |
+  Write and review meeting notes so that each one is understood by someone who missed the meeting, without trading brevity for verbosity.
+entry_condition: |
+  Meeting notes, decisions, or action items are being extracted or reviewed.
+exit_condition: |
+  Every note and decision passes the missed-the-meeting test: things and owners are named, decisions are stated, ambiguous terms are spelled out, and notes stay short.
+steps:
+- title: Apply the missed-the-meeting test
+  description: |
+    During extraction, prefer a complete short phrase over an abbreviated fragment that only makes sense with prior context. During review, test each note and decision by asking: "Would someone who missed this meeting understand this without asking a follow-up question?" If the answer is no, the note is incomplete.
+- title: Name things, decisions, and owners
+  description: |
+    Name the thing being discussed, not a pronoun for it. State the decision, not a reference to a previous conversation. Name the owner of every action, not "the team" or "TBD". If a term is ambiguous without context, spell it out.
+- title: Add missing context, not verbosity
+  description: |
+    Brevity and self-sufficiency are complementary: a well-written note is both short and complete. Do not add verbosity to achieve self-sufficiency — add the missing context instead.
diff --git a/packs/built-in/procedures/mutation-testing-review.procedure.yaml b/packs/built-in/procedures/mutation-testing-review.procedure.yaml
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..fd5fc0f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/packs/built-in/procedures/mutation-testing-review.procedure.yaml
@@ -0,0 +1,22 @@
+schema_version: '1.0'
+id: mutation-testing-review
+title: Mutation Testing Review
+description: |
+  Use mutation testing to locate weak assertions in the code under review: scope the run to the change, treat survivors as located questions, annotate genuine equivalents honestly, and read the score as a signal.
+entry_condition: |
+  A work package's tests are to be assessed for discriminating power with a mutation tool.
+exit_condition: |
+  The run covered the touched modules, each survivor was killed by an added or tightened assertion or annotated as a genuine equivalent with a reason, and no numeric target drove the work.
+steps:
+- title: Scope the run to what changed
+  description: |
+    Scope the run to what changed. Point the mutation tool at the modules the work package touched, not the whole codebase, so the run finishes inside a review cycle and every survivor it reports is about code under review.
+- title: Read each survivor as a located question
+  description: |
+    Read each surviving mutant as a located question. A survivor names a line whose mutation the tests tolerated — ask 'what behaviour should have made a test fail here?' and add or tighten the assertion that pins it, rather than deleting or muting the mutant to move the number.
+- title: Annotate genuine equivalents honestly
+  description: |
+    Annotate genuine equivalents honestly. A mutant that cannot change observable behaviour (a log string, a version constant, a formatting-only branch) is equivalent and unkillable; mark it as such with a recorded reason, and keep the equivalent rate low enough that suppression is not hiding real gaps.
+- title: Treat the score as a signal, not a goal
+  description: |
+    Treat the score as a signal, not a goal. Use the score to locate weak assertions and to watch for regressions in the suite's discriminating power; do not set a numeric target that rewards gaming, over-specification, or asserting on incidental detail.
diff --git a/packs/built-in/procedures/observable-contract-test-authoring.procedure.yaml b/packs/built-in/procedures/observable-contract-test-authoring.procedure.yaml
