@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+schema_version: '1.0'
+id: op-or-mission-lifecycle
+title: Op or Mission Lifecycle
+description: |
+  Classify work as an Op or a Mission before starting, start it through its canonical entry point, re-check the choice as the work runs, and close every Op with its real outcome.
+entry_condition: |
+  A new piece of work is about to start.
+exit_condition: |
+  The work was classified and started through its canonical entry point, an Op that surfaced a Mission trigger was closed and its remainder carried into a Mission or tracked follow-up, and every Op was closed with its real outcome.
+steps:
+- title: Classify the work against the Mission triggers
+  description: |
+    Before starting, classify the work as an Op or a Mission by applying tactic:op-or-mission-selection. If ANY Mission trigger holds, choose a Mission. The triggers are: the work needs a written spec or implementation plan; it splits into discrete, reviewable work packages; it needs an explicit review or approval gate before it lands; it will span more than one sitting; or it must be resumable by name, or by a different agent.
+- title: Otherwise choose an Op
+  description: |
+    Otherwise, choose an Op. Typical Ops are a one-off question, review or piece of advice; a small fix that needs no spec, review gate or multi-session tracking; or a request that only needs the governance context injected once.
+- title: Start through the canonical entry point
+  description: |
+    Start the chosen unit through its canonical entry point. Start an Op with `spec-kitty dispatch "<request>"`, adding `--profile <id>` when the right profile is already known. Start a Mission with `/spec-kitty.specify`. Never approximate either one by hand.
+- title: Re-check the choice while the work runs
+  description: |
+    Re-check the choice while the work runs. If an Op surfaces a Mission trigger, stop extending the Op. Two examples: the remaining scope is too large to finish cleanly in this sitting, or it needs its own design pass (a spec, an ADR or an operator decision) before any code changes. Close the Op with the outcome of the work actually done. Then carry the rest into a Mission, or into a tracked follow-up that names it.
+- title: Close every Op explicitly
+  description: |
+    Close every Op explicitly with `spec-kitty profile-invocation complete --invocation-id <id> --outcome <done|failed|abandoned>`, using the real outcome.
diff --git a/packs/built-in/procedures/read-intent-before-high-risk-operation.procedure.yaml b/packs/built-in/procedures/read-intent-before-high-risk-operation.procedure.yaml
