@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+schema_version: '1.0'
+id: red-main-handling
+title: Red Main Handling
+description: |
+  Treat a red mainline as an honest P0 signal: back accepted P0s with failing reproduction tests, gate release on green mainline CI, defer expensive QA until green, and put red recovery at the front of the queue.
+entry_condition: |
+  The mainline is red, a P0 defect is filed or accepted, or a release or QA effort is being considered.
+exit_condition: |
+  Every red traces to an accepted P0, release and expensive QA waited for green mainline CI, and red recovery was prioritized and landed through the normal PR flow after classifying each red.
+steps:
+- title: Read a red main as honest signal
+  description: |
+    Read a red main as honest signal, not a violation. Treat a red mainline as a true signal that a known release-blocking (P0) defect exists. A red main should always be traceable to an accepted P0 (a filed issue, ideally with a failing test). It's broken, we hate it, but it's the truth, so we do not hide it.
+- title: Add a failing reproduction test for a P0
+  description: |
+    On filing or accepting a P0, add a failing reproduction test. When you file or accept a P0 bug, you are free and encouraged to land a test that reproduces it: a red-first reproduction at mainline scope. A P0 that carries a failing test is unambiguous, self-documenting, and impossible to lose track of. Turning main red this way honours the process; the red is the point until the defect is fixed. This is the mainline-scope extension of the per-PR red-first / never-retry-to-green discipline.
+- title: Gate release on green mainline CI
+  description: |
+    Gate release on green mainline CI. Mainline CI status is authoritative for release readiness: red CI means no release. Because CI is the single release gate, it must mean one thing, which is exactly why green-washing is refused.
+- title: Defer expensive QA until green
+  description: |
+    Defer expensive QA and manual testing until green. There is no value in validating a base that is already known-broken; that effort waits until main is green, protecting scarce QA and human-tester time.
+- title: Prioritize red recovery
+  description: |
+    Prioritize red recovery. Green main is the goal; a red main pulls to the front of the maintainer queue. Land the fix through the normal PR flow (no-direct-push is unchanged). Classify each red before acting on it, to distinguish a genuine P0 red from a flake or pre-existing breakage while working the queue.
