+schema_version: '1.0'
+id: test-and-typecheck-gate-run
+title: Test and Typecheck Gate Run
+description: |
+  Run the full validation gate for a change before handoff: tests at the right levels, coverage, static analysis, a separate typecheck, configured compliance checks, and a clear record of pre-existing versus new failures.
+entry_condition: |
+  A work package or change is ready to be handed off for review or merge.
+exit_condition: |
+  Tests, coverage, static analysis, typecheck (with command and exit code recorded), and configured compliance checks have run for the affected scope, and pre-existing failures are recorded separately from newly introduced ones.
+steps:
+- title: Run tests at the right levels
+  description: |
+    Run unit tests for changed core logic and integration tests for changed workflow boundaries.
+- title: Meet the coverage expectation
+  description: |
+    Meet the repository coverage expectation for new code, or document the delta when pre-existing scope makes that impractical.
+- title: Run static analysis gates
+  description: |
+    Run the repository's applicable static analysis, typing, lint, or equivalent validation gates for the affected scope.
+- title: Run typecheck as a separate gate
+  description: |
+    Treat typecheck as a separate gate from the test runner. Vitest, Jest, Mocha, pytest, and similar runners do not apply TypeScript `noUnusedLocals` / `noUnusedParameters` or other compiler diagnostics. When the WP touches TypeScript (including `*.test.ts` / `*.test.tsx`), run the project's typecheck script (`npm run typecheck`, `tsc -b`, `tsc --noEmit`, or the same compile CI uses) and record the command plus exit code. A green test run is not typecheck.
+- title: Run configured compliance checks
+  description: |
+    Run configured or enabled CVE, dependency, or license-compliance checks when the repository treats them as part of the validation gate.
+- title: Separate pre-existing from new failures
+  description: |
+    Record any remaining pre-existing failures separately from newly introduced failures before handoff.
