+schema_version: '1.0'
+id: touched-area-cleanup
+title: Boy Scout Cleanup of Touched Areas
+description: |
+  Leave every area a change touches in better shape: fix the failing tests, lint findings, and type errors found there, apply minor readability and consistency cleanup, and keep that cleanup proportional and inside the change's file set.
+entry_condition: |
+  A change has opened files, and the touched area carries failing tests, lint findings, type errors, or minor readability, naming, or consistency issues.
+exit_condition: |
+  Defects surfaced in the touched area are fixed (or a genuinely broad refactor is deferred with a one-line rationale and, where useful, a tracker reference), and the cleanup stayed local to files already in the change's file set.
+steps:
+- title: Fix surfaced failures by default
+  description: |
+    When a touched area has a failing test, lint finding, or type error, fix it by default rather than deferring it or attributing it to pre-existing state.
+- title: Stay inside the change's file set
+  description: |
+    Within files already opened for the change, apply Boy Scout Rule by default: fix failing tests, lint findings, and type errors surfaced in the touched area, without expanding the file set chosen for the change.
+- title: Apply minor cleanup in touched areas
+  description: |
+    Apply minor cleanup that improves readability, naming, or consistency in touched areas.
+- title: Keep in-file cleanup unblocked by minimal-edit guidance
+  description: |
+    Tie-break: naming, readability, and consistency improvements Boy Scout Rule licenses inside the touched area are not blocked by smallest-viable-diff's minimal-edit guidance -- smallest-viable-diff governs the FILE SET chosen for the change and the size of the functional edit, not in-file naming or the minor readability cleanup Boy Scout Rule applies within files already inside that set.
+- title: Keep the fix proportional and local
+  description: |
+    Keep the fix proportional and local; defer a genuinely broad refactor (which belongs to a dedicated task) with a one-line rationale and, where useful, a tracker reference.
