+schema_version: '1.0'
+id: operator-signal-emission
+title: Operator Signal Emission
+description: |
+  Make every deciding path also signal: emit through an existing operator-visible sink, pair machine codes with a human sentence, pin the signal itself in the red-first regression, and verify it survives every seam.
+entry_condition: |
+  A code path decides something (refuses, skips, degrades, or fails) that an operator needs to know about.
+exit_condition: |
+  The decision emits through an existing operator-visible sink with a human sentence beside the machine-readable signal, a red-first regression asserts on the emitted signal, and the human half is verified to survive every seam to the operator surface.
+steps:
+- title: Emit through an existing operator-visible sink
+  description: |
+    Emit the human-readable half through an EXISTING operator-visible sink (CLI output, doctor/status JSON, the event log, a non-zero exit code). Never route the signal through a new bespoke sink that can itself be swallowed — reuse a surface operators already watch, because per epics #3410/#3549 an un-watched sink is indistinguishable from no signal.
+- title: Pair every machine signal with a human sentence
+  description: |
+    Pair every machine-readable signal with a human sentence that states plainly what happened and what the operator should do next. A correct error_code with no accompanying human sentence is a silent-drop, not a fail-loud.
+- title: Assert on the emitted signal in the red-first regression
+  description: |
+    Write the red-first regression so it asserts on the emitted SIGNAL — the operator-visible line, the JSON field, the log record — not merely on the machine code or the byte-shape of the artifact. A test that only checks the shape passes vacuously while the human half is dropped, which is the exact fake-green failure mode this contract closes.
+- title: Verify the human half survives every seam
+  description: |
+    When a decision path and its reporting surface are separated by a seam, verify at the seam that the human half survives the crossing; a signal that is emitted upstream but discarded before it reaches the operator surface still violates this contract.
